{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2603.16646, what are two main concerns educators have about generative AI's representation of Hawaiian culture, and how do these concerns relate to student learning?", "answer": "Educators are concerned about hallucinated outputs with culturally incorrect information and about AI providing a superficial, 'mainland' perspective of Hawaiian culture, both of which can negatively impact student learning and perpetuate stereotypes.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "participants were especially concerned about the tangible repercussions hallucinated outputs could have on their students' education."}, {"page": 9, "quote": "Participants reported that AI outputs often provided a 'mainland' perspective of Hawaiian culture that was incongruous with students' lived experiences and could even perpetuate harmful stereotypes."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2603.16646, how do participants' views on source attribution in AI auditing tools connect to concerns about Indigenous data sovereignty?", "answer": "Participants want source attribution to include knowledge genealogy, but also stress that any collected cultural data must remain under community control to prevent misappropriation and uphold Indigenous data sovereignty.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "this tool would require creating and maintaining a corpus of culturally validated material, as well as require users to contribute to its source genealogy, would require significant community labor and governance structures for who is allowed to contribute validated sources."}, {"page": 10, "quote": "for this design to be successful, it most not only integrate with Hawaiian epistemologies that center knowledge provenance, but also uphold data sovereignty, ensuring any source material or cultural interpretations collected remains in the hands of the community."}]},

  {"question": "What are the three main categories of AI auditing tools ideated by participants in arXiv 2603.16646, as illustrated in Figure 5?", "answer": "The three categories are tracing sources, identifying perspectives in generated outputs, and flagging instances of harmful content.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "these include: (1) tracing sources, (2) identifying perspectives in generated outputs, and (3) flagging instances of harmful content. While these tools resemble existing, general-purpose approaches for auditing or mitigating hallucinations in generative AI, our findings show that participants' designs are shaped by the cultural context and educational setting."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2603.16646, what does the term 'genealogy of knowledge' mean in the context of source attribution for Hawaiian cultural content?", "answer": "It refers to tracing the lineage of a source's author, including who taught them, to assess trustworthiness, rather than relying solely on citations.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "participants in Workshop 4 proposed an auditing tool that allows users to trace the genealogy of a linked source, displaying information about the author as well as the lineage of their teachers."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2603.16646, what is a reported example of AI-generated content that failed to reflect the diversity of Hawai'i's population?", "answer": "Generated images of classrooms depicted only Polynesians, ignoring the presence of white students who make up about 30% of the population.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "when observing generated images of classrooms in Hawaii', 'these pictures all seem to just be like Polynesians, where Hawai'i is so much more than just Polynesians.' Similarly, a participant from Workshop 1 commented that 'it' s interesting that there ' s not a white child... like the assumption there that everybody here is of Hawaiian descent or even a mix, but look at our school population - many students are white, right? Like 30% or something.'"}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2603.16646, what is one reason educators felt AI-generated outputs about Hawaiian culture were not trustworthy, even when written in the Hawaiian language?", "answer": "Because even Hawaiian-language sources can have contrasting editorial stances, so knowing the author's background is crucial for trust.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "Workshop 4 participants noted that even Hawaiian-language newspapers offered contrasting editorial stances depending on who was in charge. As one participant explained, 'Sometimes when you look at the Hawaiian newspaper and you read it, you ' re like, what am I reading? You know, it' s all in Hawaiian, but they ' re telling you, oh, ' surfing is bad' .\""}]}
]}
